1
00:01:13,073 --> 00:01:15,668
Oh, you could take a
week and go to Lake Louise.

2
00:01:15,842 --> 00:01:18,175
- Oh.
- Oh, look at that scenery.

3
00:01:18,345 --> 00:01:22,749
Snow-capped
mountains, pristine lakes.

4
00:01:22,916 --> 00:01:24,646
Does it say how much it is?

5
00:01:24,818 --> 00:01:26,878
That's not important.

6
00:01:27,087 --> 00:01:29,647
The Greek islands. Oh,
Dad, you could take a cruise.

7
00:01:29,823 --> 00:01:30,847
Oh.

8
00:01:31,024 --> 00:01:33,858
- Oh, would you look at the water?
- Oh, yeah.

9
00:01:34,027 --> 00:01:36,087
Beautiful blue.

10
00:01:36,263 --> 00:01:37,595
There's a price on the back.

11
00:01:37,764 --> 00:01:41,223
Dad, life's expensive,
then you die.

12
00:01:41,401 --> 00:01:43,495
You can't take it with you.

13
00:01:44,438 --> 00:01:46,532
I could try.

14
00:01:47,774 --> 00:01:49,402
I could do like Rochester

15
00:01:49,576 --> 00:01:52,375
said Jack Benny on
the radio was gonna do.

16
00:01:52,546 --> 00:01:54,378
And what, pray tell, was that?

17
00:01:54,715 --> 00:01:57,412
Sew it to my shroud.

18
00:02:02,322 --> 00:02:04,257
Hello, Ben.

19
00:02:05,225 --> 00:02:07,285
You could knock.

20
00:02:22,476 --> 00:02:25,139
Billy, are you all right?

21
00:02:31,518 --> 00:02:33,316
Lucy's dead.

22
00:02:34,755 --> 00:02:36,519
Lucy's dead?

23
00:02:36,690 --> 00:02:38,750
Lucy's dead.

24
00:02:38,925 --> 00:02:40,553
Died last night in her sleep

25
00:02:40,727 --> 00:02:43,629
in that beach house she
had up in North Carolina.

26
00:02:44,498 --> 00:02:47,627
Some kid who's renting
a room from her found her

27
00:02:47,968 --> 00:02:50,096
and called me about an hour ago.

28
00:02:50,937 --> 00:02:54,704
Oh, Billy, I'm so sorry.

29
00:02:54,875 --> 00:02:56,571
Lucy's dead.

30
00:02:56,743 --> 00:02:58,871
Lucy's dead.

31
00:02:59,546 --> 00:03:02,516
My dear, beloved sister.

32
00:03:04,751 --> 00:03:06,913
Why is it you're never
grateful for what you've got

33
00:03:07,087 --> 00:03:09,352
until you don't
have it any more?

34
00:03:09,523 --> 00:03:11,424
Hit me driving over here.

35
00:03:11,758 --> 00:03:14,387
I took her for granted, Ben.

36
00:03:14,561 --> 00:03:17,861
I don't even remember
what color her eyes were.

37
00:03:18,165 --> 00:03:19,827
They were brown.

38
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:21,024
How would you know?

39
00:03:21,835 --> 00:03:23,997
I went with her all
through high school.

40
00:03:25,238 --> 00:03:26,638
And then you dumped her.

41
00:03:27,841 --> 00:03:29,139
I didn't dump her.

42
00:03:29,309 --> 00:03:30,971
You dumped her.

43
00:03:31,144 --> 00:03:33,113
She wound up living
with me for 35 years

44
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:36,011
because you dumped
her and broke her heart.

45
00:03:36,316 --> 00:03:39,081
Now neither of us know
the color of her eyes.

46
00:03:39,519 --> 00:03:40,987
They were brown.

47
00:03:41,455 --> 00:03:43,947
Funeral's day after tomorrow.

48
00:03:44,524 --> 00:03:46,322
Lucy's dead.

49
00:03:47,994 --> 00:03:50,429
Sooner we leave for
North Carolina, the better.

50
00:03:50,597 --> 00:03:53,431
Yeah. It'll be a
long drive for you.

51
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:54,863
You're gonna drive.

52
00:03:55,669 --> 00:03:56,693
What?

53
00:03:56,870 --> 00:04:00,068
- You're gonna drive. Your car.
- Why?

54
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:02,300
Well, I can't take my pickup.

55
00:04:02,476 --> 00:04:05,036
The boy's car would never
make it out of the state.

56
00:04:06,213 --> 00:04:07,681
Why are you looking
at me like that?

57
00:04:07,848 --> 00:04:10,408
You were going
anyway, weren't you?

58
00:04:10,917 --> 00:04:14,012
I mean, if you got time to go
on some kind of fancy cruise,

59
00:04:14,187 --> 00:04:16,053
you surely got time to see to it

60
00:04:16,223 --> 00:04:18,886
that the girl who spent half
her life pining away for you

61
00:04:19,059 --> 00:04:20,687
gets a proper send-off.

62
00:04:22,329 --> 00:04:25,265
It's all right, Dad. I'll take
care of everything right here.

63
00:04:25,432 --> 00:04:27,298
No.

64
00:04:28,769 --> 00:04:35,232
I can't pick out a burial
dress or arrange flowers

65
00:04:35,609 --> 00:04:38,738
or do a get-together
after the funeral.

66
00:04:41,081 --> 00:04:43,107
You're coming too.

67
00:05:21,121 --> 00:05:23,283
We should almost be there.

68
00:05:24,257 --> 00:05:25,281
Oh.

69
00:05:25,458 --> 00:05:28,724
This is where Lucy
spent her last days.

70
00:05:29,763 --> 00:05:33,222
Some rundown shack on the beach.

71
00:05:35,836 --> 00:05:37,327
It's all my fault.

72
00:05:37,504 --> 00:05:39,769
Would you stop beating
up on yourself, Pop?

73
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:42,100
She's the one who sold the
farm out from underneath you

74
00:05:42,242 --> 00:05:43,540
and ran off with half the money.

75
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:45,372
She made you
miserable. You know it.

76
00:05:45,545 --> 00:05:48,481
Yeah. She had it tough.

77
00:05:48,648 --> 00:05:52,676
Good Lord didn't exactly endow
her with looks and brains, you know.

78
00:05:52,853 --> 00:05:56,085
About the only fellow who
looked at her twice was Ben.

79
00:05:56,256 --> 00:05:58,816
Tells you something right there.

80
00:05:59,326 --> 00:06:01,386
You gonna eat that pickle?

81
00:06:04,331 --> 00:06:07,028
Here's the driveway, Pop.
Right here. Turn here. Turn here.

82
00:06:30,056 --> 00:06:32,287
This is Lucy's house?

83
00:06:32,459 --> 00:06:34,485
I guess so.

84
00:06:46,406 --> 00:06:47,704
How long did she own this place?

85
00:06:47,874 --> 00:06:49,638
Uh, about two years, I think.

86
00:06:49,809 --> 00:06:52,438
- What was your name, again?
- Clyde. Clyde Eller.

87
00:06:52,612 --> 00:06:55,309
I knew her from the
surfboard shop where I work.

88
00:06:55,916 --> 00:06:58,545
What was Lucy doing
in a surfboard shop?

89
00:06:58,718 --> 00:07:00,084
She hung out there all the time.

90
00:07:00,253 --> 00:07:02,518
I was paying for my room
by teaching her how to paddle.

91
00:07:02,689 --> 00:07:04,988
Aunt Lucy was
learning how to surf?

92
00:07:05,158 --> 00:07:06,990
- Yeah. BILLY: Are
you sure we're talking

93
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:09,129
about the same Lucy Lewis?

94
00:07:09,296 --> 00:07:13,961
About this fella's age,
about yay tall and yay wide.

95
00:07:14,134 --> 00:07:15,762
Well, you tell me.

96
00:07:15,936 --> 00:07:17,802
Here's her picture.

97
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:24,776
- That's her, all right.
- No, it's not.

98
00:07:24,945 --> 00:07:26,573
Yes, it is. Look, brown eyes.

99
00:07:26,746 --> 00:07:28,612
She got orange in her air.

100
00:07:28,782 --> 00:07:31,616
- What's she wearing on her feet?
- That looks like Rollerblades.

101
00:07:31,785 --> 00:07:33,481
Yeah, she was something.

102
00:07:33,653 --> 00:07:36,452
Game for anything. Loved
having young people around.

103
00:07:36,623 --> 00:07:38,023
Door was always open.

104
00:07:38,191 --> 00:07:40,422
Fridge always stocked.

105
00:07:40,593 --> 00:07:42,755
She was on drugs, wasn't she?

106
00:07:42,929 --> 00:07:45,421
No way. Wouldn't allow
booze in the house either.

107
00:07:45,598 --> 00:07:49,399
Nope. She was just out
to have a good, clean time.

108
00:07:50,003 --> 00:07:52,203
Well, listen, I'm gonna take
a swim before I go to work.

109
00:07:52,339 --> 00:07:54,706
There's two bedrooms on
this level and three above

110
00:07:54,874 --> 00:07:57,639
and one below, not including
mine, so you all have a good time.

111
00:07:57,811 --> 00:07:59,871
- Okay?
- Thanks. We will.

112
00:08:00,046 --> 00:08:01,241
See you.

113
00:08:03,850 --> 00:08:07,810
One good thing. We don't
have to pay for a motel.

114
00:08:09,222 --> 00:08:10,884
Let's go get the luggage.
- Okay.

115
00:08:23,036 --> 00:08:24,868
Damn.

116
00:08:27,507 --> 00:08:29,567
Hey, wait for me!

117
00:08:39,753 --> 00:08:41,688
Where'd she go?

118
00:08:43,556 --> 00:08:45,548
See you later!

119
00:08:48,228 --> 00:08:50,697
- How's this?
- Good.

120
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:56,360
- New kid on the block.
- I'm over here!

121
00:09:02,709 --> 00:09:03,733
Nice car.

122
00:09:08,648 --> 00:09:10,048
Nice everything.

123
00:09:11,217 --> 00:09:13,516
- Things are looking up.
- Ha, ha.

124
00:09:23,863 --> 00:09:24,887
Hey, Carrie.

125
00:09:27,067 --> 00:09:28,592
Things were looking up.

126
00:09:29,002 --> 00:09:30,595
Oh, no!

127
00:09:38,011 --> 00:09:41,175
Hey, Ashley. This yours?

128
00:09:41,714 --> 00:09:42,841
Yeah. How'd you get it?

129
00:09:43,016 --> 00:09:46,418
Somebody found it over
by the volleyball court.

130
00:09:46,753 --> 00:09:48,415
I remember seeing you wear it.

131
00:09:49,089 --> 00:09:50,557
Mm.

132
00:09:51,324 --> 00:09:52,348
Thanks.

133
00:10:01,634 --> 00:10:03,398
Hey, how you doing?

134
00:10:03,570 --> 00:10:05,038
Tim, right? TIM: Right.

135
00:10:05,205 --> 00:10:08,437
- How's the new board working out?
- Oh. First chance I had to try it.

136
00:10:08,608 --> 00:10:10,577
Well, be careful. Undertow's
pretty heavy today.

137
00:10:11,778 --> 00:10:13,041
Flag's not up.

138
00:10:16,950 --> 00:10:18,816
Yeah, well, I, uh...

139
00:10:18,985 --> 00:10:22,649
I guess our fearless lifeguard
has other things on his mind.

140
00:10:24,090 --> 00:10:25,956
I'll catch you later.

141
00:10:30,396 --> 00:10:31,921
To the right.

142
00:10:38,371 --> 00:10:39,999
Hey, Mike.

143
00:10:40,707 --> 00:10:42,747
Look, I don't mean to tell
you your job or anything,

144
00:10:42,876 --> 00:10:45,539
but the undertow's
getting pretty bad out there.

145
00:10:45,712 --> 00:10:48,978
You might wanna do
something with this.

146
00:10:52,085 --> 00:10:54,816
Oh, you mean, like,
uh, stick it someplace?

147
00:10:55,688 --> 00:10:57,623
Yeah, that's
exactly what I mean.

148
00:10:57,790 --> 00:10:59,520
All right. How about...?

149
00:11:00,059 --> 00:11:02,119
- There. How's that? Huh?
- Hey.

150
00:11:05,365 --> 00:11:06,765
Hey.

151
00:11:08,801 --> 00:11:10,360
Jerk.

152
00:11:14,941 --> 00:11:17,740
Why don't you leave
Clyde alone for a change?

153
00:11:32,192 --> 00:11:33,592
Mr. Lewis.

154
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:35,695
I'm so sorry to have
kept you waiting.

155
00:11:35,862 --> 00:11:37,956
It's all right, Reverend.

156
00:11:38,131 --> 00:11:40,362
This is a friend of
mine, Ben Matlock.

157
00:11:40,533 --> 00:11:42,934
Virgil Atkinson. Nice to
meet you, Mr. Matlock.

158
00:11:43,102 --> 00:11:45,867
Lord knows Billy
needs a friend right now.

159
00:11:46,039 --> 00:11:47,063
I knew Lucy too.

160
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:50,267
Kind, generous,
happy-go-lucky Lucy.

161
00:11:50,443 --> 00:11:52,639
Another reason I wanted
to see you, Mr. Lewis,

162
00:11:52,812 --> 00:11:55,748
is that I was hoping that you could
give me some stories about Lucy

163
00:11:55,915 --> 00:11:57,975
that I could use at
the funeral service.

164
00:11:58,151 --> 00:12:00,484
- Stories.
- You know, anecdotes.

165
00:12:00,653 --> 00:12:02,485
Perhaps something
she did as a little girl

166
00:12:02,655 --> 00:12:04,590
that was particularly memorable

167
00:12:04,757 --> 00:12:08,387
or indicative of the
kind of person she was.

168
00:12:08,561 --> 00:12:11,258
Well, you tell me
when to stop, Reverend,

169
00:12:11,431 --> 00:12:13,764
because there was no
one this side of the Jordan

170
00:12:13,933 --> 00:12:17,131
that did more good
works than my sister.

171
00:12:17,303 --> 00:12:20,239
Like the time she found
a nest of thrush eggs

172
00:12:20,406 --> 00:12:23,137
in a hayfield our pa just mowed.

173
00:12:24,510 --> 00:12:26,445
Get out of there!

174
00:12:28,915 --> 00:12:30,042
You all right?

175
00:12:30,216 --> 00:12:31,741
Wave knocked me
down. I banged my knee.

176
00:12:31,918 --> 00:12:33,216
You need some help?

177
00:12:33,386 --> 00:12:34,945
Thanks.

178
00:12:36,055 --> 00:12:37,148
Agh.

179
00:12:37,323 --> 00:12:39,724
- Thanks a lot. You're a sweetheart.
- No problem.

180
00:12:39,892 --> 00:12:41,918
- What's your name?
- Carrie.

181
00:12:42,095 --> 00:12:44,621
- I'm Tim.
- Hi.

182
00:12:53,506 --> 00:12:57,534
And this is our
top-of-the-line model.

183
00:12:57,710 --> 00:13:01,511
Polished cherry
wood, silk lining.

184
00:13:01,681 --> 00:13:06,710
Nylon padding,
solid brass fittings.

185
00:13:06,886 --> 00:13:09,856
Very luxurious.

186
00:13:10,123 --> 00:13:11,147
How much?

187
00:13:12,225 --> 00:13:16,321
Well, the first one we
looked at runs around 1,500.

188
00:13:16,496 --> 00:13:19,193
This one goes for 3,000.

189
00:13:19,899 --> 00:13:26,396
And the other three we looked
at, 1,900, 2,000 and 2,500.

190
00:13:26,572 --> 00:13:29,974
Three thousand dollars for something
you're gonna stick in the ground

191
00:13:30,143 --> 00:13:31,634
and throw dirt on?

192
00:13:31,811 --> 00:13:35,304
Most people want the best for
their loved ones, Mr. Matlock.

193
00:13:35,481 --> 00:13:37,882
As a final gesture of respect.

194
00:13:40,353 --> 00:13:42,413
It's for dear, sweet Lucy, Ben.

195
00:13:42,588 --> 00:13:45,422
Well, Billy, she'll never
know the difference.

196
00:13:48,561 --> 00:13:50,553
It's your money.

197
00:13:52,699 --> 00:13:56,033
The one for 3,000.

198
00:13:57,537 --> 00:14:00,006
You, uh, take a check?

199
00:14:00,306 --> 00:14:03,868
Not from an out-of-state
bank, I'm afraid.

200
00:14:12,685 --> 00:14:14,244
Pay him.

201
00:14:17,323 --> 00:14:18,347
Me?

202
00:14:18,524 --> 00:14:22,689
Use one of those fancy credit
cards you're always flashing.

203
00:14:24,197 --> 00:14:28,430
- Billy...
- I want Lucy to have the best.

204
00:14:41,147 --> 00:14:43,707
Like the time
when, as a little girl,

205
00:14:43,883 --> 00:14:47,911
she found a nest of eggs on the
ground in a newly-mowed field,

206
00:14:48,087 --> 00:14:50,989
and she watched and
waited for the mother bird.

207
00:14:51,157 --> 00:14:52,386
But when it became apparent

208
00:14:52,558 --> 00:14:55,289
that she had abandoned
her fallen nest,

209
00:14:55,461 --> 00:14:57,123
Lucy took it home

210
00:14:57,296 --> 00:15:01,063
and tried day and night
to keep the eggs warm,

211
00:15:01,234 --> 00:15:04,170
in hopes that they
would hatch. That was...

212
00:15:04,337 --> 00:15:06,329
- No, she didn't.
- Shh.

213
00:15:06,506 --> 00:15:09,908
She scrambled them up
and ate them for breakfast.

214
00:15:10,076 --> 00:15:12,705
Lucy had a mean streak.

215
00:15:12,879 --> 00:15:16,816
Billy's acting like she's the
second coming of Mother Teresa.

216
00:15:16,983 --> 00:15:18,918
It's his way of
dealing with his grief.

217
00:15:19,085 --> 00:15:21,748
This whole time she lived
in our little community,

218
00:15:21,921 --> 00:15:23,651
- she filled our minds - Sure.

219
00:15:23,823 --> 00:15:27,316
With richness and humor,
personality and love.

220
00:15:27,493 --> 00:15:30,861
Lucy Lewis has undoubtedly
left us for a better place.

221
00:15:31,030 --> 00:15:32,550
Her spirit, however,
will remain here.

222
00:15:41,441 --> 00:15:43,933
So you come here every summer?

223
00:15:44,110 --> 00:15:45,271
Yeah, just about.

224
00:15:45,445 --> 00:15:47,539
My aunt has a place here.

225
00:15:47,814 --> 00:15:49,339
Where are you staying?

226
00:15:49,515 --> 00:15:50,608
In my car.

227
00:15:52,418 --> 00:15:53,818
They do have hotels around here.

228
00:15:53,986 --> 00:15:55,750
I came here to surf.
I don't need a room.

229
00:15:55,922 --> 00:15:57,015
Ah.

230
00:15:57,190 --> 00:15:58,283
What do you want?

231
00:15:58,458 --> 00:16:02,862
Although I may end up staying
a little longer than I thought.

232
00:16:05,698 --> 00:16:07,997
- Want another pitcher?
- Mm.

233
00:16:09,402 --> 00:16:10,927
I'm getting tired of this place.

234
00:16:11,103 --> 00:16:13,368
You wanna go someplace else?

235
00:16:13,873 --> 00:16:15,307
My place?

236
00:16:16,409 --> 00:16:17,707
Your aunt won't mind?

237
00:16:17,877 --> 00:16:19,778
My aunt is in Europe
for the summer.

238
00:16:20,346 --> 00:16:23,282
And my roommate's
having dinner downtown.

239
00:17:04,123 --> 00:17:05,682
Tim, don't.

240
00:17:08,828 --> 00:17:10,558
Please.

241
00:17:11,364 --> 00:17:13,230
Why?

242
00:17:18,437 --> 00:17:19,598
Tim, no.

243
00:17:19,772 --> 00:17:21,741
Please, stop.

244
00:17:31,117 --> 00:17:33,348
Tim, don't.

245
00:17:37,056 --> 00:17:38,490
Damn it! I said, stop it!

246
00:17:38,658 --> 00:17:40,422
What is wrong with you?
- Take it easy.

247
00:17:40,593 --> 00:17:42,858
- Don't touch me!
- What's going on?

248
00:17:44,764 --> 00:17:47,290
I told him to stop, but he wouldn't.
- Hey, hey. What's...?

249
00:17:47,466 --> 00:17:49,196
Get away!

250
00:17:54,807 --> 00:17:55,968
I didn't do anything.

251
00:17:56,142 --> 00:17:58,077
I said, leave.

252
00:17:59,478 --> 00:18:00,878
Uh...

253
00:18:45,992 --> 00:18:47,483
Hi.

254
00:18:48,094 --> 00:18:51,861
In case Carrie didn't
mention it, my name's Ashley.

255
00:18:53,432 --> 00:18:55,731
You got one crazy
roommate, Ashley.

256
00:18:55,901 --> 00:18:58,268
I thought it only
fair to warn you.

257
00:18:58,437 --> 00:19:00,906
Last night, after you
left, she called the police.

258
00:19:01,874 --> 00:19:04,673
She wanted to know what
constitutes attempted rape.

259
00:19:11,484 --> 00:19:12,747
She didn't tell them your name,

260
00:19:12,918 --> 00:19:15,752
but she's thinking of
having you arrested.

261
00:19:17,623 --> 00:19:19,524
I didn't try to rape her.

262
00:19:19,692 --> 00:19:21,991
She says you did. And, frankly,

263
00:19:23,162 --> 00:19:25,256
from what I saw, if anybody
asked, I'd have to agree.

264
00:19:25,431 --> 00:19:26,729
Oh, man.

265
00:19:26,899 --> 00:19:28,731
I don't believe this.

266
00:19:28,901 --> 00:19:31,962
- Look, Carrie's got a few problems.
- Yeah, tell me about it.

267
00:19:32,138 --> 00:19:35,199
Pressing charges will
only screw her up more.

268
00:19:35,374 --> 00:19:37,974
I'm gonna suggest something
that may seem a little crass at first,

269
00:19:38,144 --> 00:19:40,238
but hear me out, okay?

270
00:19:41,280 --> 00:19:44,148
Carrie's deeply in
debt. She needs money.

271
00:19:44,316 --> 00:19:45,579
What are you saying?

272
00:19:45,751 --> 00:19:47,413
You're saying that I
should pay her off?

273
00:19:47,586 --> 00:19:49,953
I'm saying that if you were
to do something to show her

274
00:19:50,122 --> 00:19:51,362
that you're not such a bad guy,

275
00:19:51,991 --> 00:19:54,187
that you're honestly
sorry for what happened,

276
00:19:54,360 --> 00:19:57,125
and then just quietly left town,

277
00:19:57,530 --> 00:20:00,125
she'd probably be willing
to drop the whole thing.

278
00:20:06,205 --> 00:20:08,299
It's just a suggestion.

279
00:20:17,416 --> 00:20:19,112
Carrie.

280
00:20:19,351 --> 00:20:21,513
Did you find him? - Yup.

281
00:20:29,962 --> 00:20:31,157
How much?

282
00:20:33,065 --> 00:20:34,260
Three grand.

283
00:20:36,135 --> 00:20:37,933
Yes.

284
00:20:52,184 --> 00:20:53,675
Thanks.

285
00:20:57,723 --> 00:20:58,747
All the way.

286
00:20:58,924 --> 00:21:00,290
- Cookie, anyone?
- Oh, yeah.

287
00:21:00,459 --> 00:21:02,621
- Oh, no, thanks.
- Oh, go ahead, you'll swim it off.

288
00:21:02,795 --> 00:21:05,959
Oh, no, I'm diabetic.
I can't have sugar.

289
00:21:06,298 --> 00:21:08,529
- Hey, Clyde.
- Hey, C.J. How you doing?

290
00:21:08,701 --> 00:21:10,727
- Uh, excuse me.
- Mm-hm.

291
00:21:11,670 --> 00:21:14,265
I can't get over how many
young people are here

292
00:21:14,440 --> 00:21:16,671
and how they were
all so fond of Lucy.

293
00:21:16,842 --> 00:21:20,836
She must have gone back to
being the girl I knew in high school.

294
00:21:21,013 --> 00:21:23,744
Tell me the truth,
Dad, did you love her?

295
00:21:24,216 --> 00:21:28,114
Well... Yeah, I guess.

296
00:21:28,287 --> 00:21:30,779
So how come you
didn't marry her?

297
00:21:31,257 --> 00:21:34,489
Well, she was two
years older than me,

298
00:21:34,660 --> 00:21:38,062
and I could see down
the road, you know.

299
00:21:44,336 --> 00:21:45,429
Where you going, Pop?

300
00:21:45,604 --> 00:21:47,539
Outside.

301
00:21:59,118 --> 00:22:00,381
You all right, Pop?

302
00:22:02,388 --> 00:22:05,051
I got a lot on my mind, Cliff.

303
00:22:06,826 --> 00:22:10,729
Oh, it's weird, you know.
It's just you and me now.

304
00:22:13,365 --> 00:22:14,799
Come on, Pop, talk to me.

305
00:22:14,967 --> 00:22:16,526
Open up.

306
00:22:18,070 --> 00:22:19,902
Okay.

307
00:22:20,239 --> 00:22:22,231
I got a question for you, son.

308
00:22:24,076 --> 00:22:25,806
Anything.

309
00:22:28,914 --> 00:22:31,782
Who do you think
she left this house to?

310
00:23:23,202 --> 00:23:26,434
So then he says, "You
mean, I should pay her off?"

311
00:23:26,605 --> 00:23:27,800
Wow.

312
00:23:27,973 --> 00:23:30,943
I mean, he got this
pathetic look on his face.

313
00:23:31,110 --> 00:23:33,270
Like a puppy that somebody
just kicked across the room.

314
00:23:33,412 --> 00:23:35,847
And then three
seconds later, he says:

315
00:23:36,015 --> 00:23:37,745
- "How much?"
- Oh, God.

316
00:23:37,917 --> 00:23:40,512
I mean, usually, they stomp
around all indignant and mad.

317
00:23:40,686 --> 00:23:42,052
This one, instant cave.

318
00:23:42,221 --> 00:23:44,918
Should've asked for more.
- I don't think he had it.

319
00:24:06,378 --> 00:24:07,971
Mm.

320
00:24:08,714 --> 00:24:10,046
What kind of fish is this?

321
00:24:10,215 --> 00:24:12,684
- Sea bass.
- It's good.

322
00:24:12,851 --> 00:24:14,444
I'm beginning to
like it here, Leanne.

323
00:24:14,620 --> 00:24:16,350
That's great.

324
00:24:16,522 --> 00:24:17,888
Maybe I'll take surfing lessons.

325
00:24:18,057 --> 00:24:19,857
How you doing? - Give me a beer.

326
00:24:19,992 --> 00:24:22,723
- Whatever's on tap.
- You got it.

327
00:24:22,962 --> 00:24:25,193
Hey, Tim. How's it going?

328
00:24:25,364 --> 00:24:27,196
Don't ask.

329
00:24:30,269 --> 00:24:32,829
Tim, come over.

330
00:24:33,973 --> 00:24:36,841
Uh, Leanne Mclntyre,
Cliff Lewis, this is Tim...

331
00:24:37,009 --> 00:24:38,033
- McDaniels.
- Hey.

332
00:24:38,210 --> 00:24:39,234
Hi.

333
00:24:39,411 --> 00:24:40,845
Leanne and Cliff are lawyers.

334
00:24:41,013 --> 00:24:44,040
Came up from Atlanta
for Lucy Lewis' funeral.

335
00:24:44,216 --> 00:24:45,650
Did you know her too?

336
00:24:45,818 --> 00:24:49,385
No. Um... What kind of lawyers?

337
00:24:49,555 --> 00:24:50,750
Criminal, mostly.

338
00:24:52,591 --> 00:24:54,856
Do you mind if I ask you
a hypothetical question?

339
00:24:55,027 --> 00:24:56,222
Go ahead.

340
00:24:56,395 --> 00:24:58,523
Okay. Look.

341
00:24:59,331 --> 00:25:03,393
Say I have a friend, and
say he meets this girl,

342
00:25:03,569 --> 00:25:07,665
buys her a few beers, they hit it off,
and he ends up at her place, okay?

343
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:09,809
- Okay. TIM:
Well, say, they're...

344
00:25:09,975 --> 00:25:12,001
They're, uh... They're
fooling around.

345
00:25:12,344 --> 00:25:14,210
But before anything
serious starts to happen,

346
00:25:14,380 --> 00:25:16,474
she starts screaming the
guy's trying to rape her.

347
00:25:16,648 --> 00:25:18,617
I mean, he isn't,
but she claims he is,

348
00:25:18,784 --> 00:25:22,221
and she's making noise,
like she's gonna call the cops.

349
00:25:22,388 --> 00:25:25,187
Well, at this point, the guy
figures that she's a little cracked.

350
00:25:25,357 --> 00:25:30,091
So to smooth
things over, he, uh...

351
00:25:30,596 --> 00:25:32,360
He pays her off.

352
00:25:32,531 --> 00:25:35,763
Only then he discovers that
she and her roommate set him up.

353
00:25:35,934 --> 00:25:37,493
The whole thing was a scam.

354
00:25:37,669 --> 00:25:40,468
They extort money from guys
like him, as a matter of course.

355
00:25:40,639 --> 00:25:41,971
Nice girls.

356
00:25:42,141 --> 00:25:46,602
Well, my question is:
What can he do about it?

357
00:25:46,779 --> 00:25:48,659
Unless you can prove the
whole thing was a scam,

358
00:25:48,814 --> 00:25:49,975
there isn't much you can do.

359
00:25:50,349 --> 00:25:51,578
How does he prove it?

360
00:25:52,051 --> 00:25:54,782
Well, he could try to track
down some of their other victims,

361
00:25:54,953 --> 00:25:56,833
see if any of them are
willing to press charges.

362
00:25:56,989 --> 00:25:59,925
Or he could wait till they try it
again and catch them in the act.

363
00:26:00,092 --> 00:26:01,754
As it were.

364
00:26:02,761 --> 00:26:04,252
Yeah.

365
00:26:04,696 --> 00:26:06,221
Okay.

366
00:26:18,210 --> 00:26:19,542
- Mr. Lewis?
- You Lucy's lawyer?

367
00:26:19,711 --> 00:26:22,271
Yes. My name is Phil...

368
00:26:26,552 --> 00:26:29,147
- Oh, I thought I heard the doorbell.
- Lucy's lawyer.

369
00:26:29,321 --> 00:26:31,688
- Hi, I'm Phil Trenton.
- Ben Matlock. Want a soda?

370
00:26:31,857 --> 00:26:35,055
You don't have to treat him
like a guest, Ben, he's a lawyer.

371
00:26:35,227 --> 00:26:36,490
Where's the will?

372
00:26:36,662 --> 00:26:40,099
I have a copy of it right here.

373
00:26:41,667 --> 00:26:45,331
Who'd, uh, she
leave this house to?

374
00:26:45,504 --> 00:26:47,598
Well, let's see.

375
00:26:47,773 --> 00:26:48,797
Ah.

376
00:26:49,074 --> 00:26:52,408
To a Mr. Clyde Eller.

377
00:26:53,412 --> 00:26:54,505
Clyde Eller?

378
00:26:54,680 --> 00:26:56,706
Ha, ha, Clyde Eller? Ha-ha-ha.

379
00:26:56,882 --> 00:26:59,283
She can't do that. Ben,
tell him she can't do that.

380
00:26:59,451 --> 00:27:01,283
She bought this
house with my money.

381
00:27:01,453 --> 00:27:04,150
She can't turn around and leave
it to someone who's not even kin.

382
00:27:04,923 --> 00:27:06,858
How could she do this?

383
00:27:07,759 --> 00:27:09,557
She can't do this.

384
00:27:09,728 --> 00:27:11,390
There's no justice.

385
00:27:11,563 --> 00:27:14,465
I loved her better
than life itself.

386
00:27:15,534 --> 00:27:17,162
- It's not fair.
- Billy.

387
00:27:17,336 --> 00:27:19,976
It's your fault. If you'd married
her the way you were supposed to,

388
00:27:20,139 --> 00:27:23,075
she wouldn't have moved down here,
left this house to some snot-nosed kid.

389
00:27:23,242 --> 00:27:26,041
I listened to her
whine all those years.

390
00:27:26,211 --> 00:27:28,237
Year after year after year,

391
00:27:28,413 --> 00:27:30,609
complaining about
backaches and bellyaches

392
00:27:30,782 --> 00:27:32,614
and toe aches.

393
00:27:32,784 --> 00:27:35,264
I had to go out to the barn to
sneak a little drink of whiskey.

394
00:27:35,420 --> 00:27:38,151
I had to keep my head to the
side so she couldn't smell it.

395
00:27:38,323 --> 00:27:41,452
I put up with that old
hag for 35 years. Damn!

396
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:43,695
It's got to be my house.
- Billy.

397
00:27:44,096 --> 00:27:45,689
Keep out of this.

398
00:27:45,864 --> 00:27:47,833
She did have other assets.

399
00:27:48,700 --> 00:27:49,724
Who gets those?

400
00:27:49,902 --> 00:27:53,236
Well, uh, she left her
Bible, some letters,

401
00:27:53,405 --> 00:27:56,671
some photos, and
several scrapbooks to, uh...

402
00:27:56,842 --> 00:27:59,404
Oh, and I quote,
"The man I loved

403
00:27:59,578 --> 00:28:03,345
back when I didn't
have a brain in my head.

404
00:28:03,515 --> 00:28:04,539
Ben Matlock."

405
00:28:04,716 --> 00:28:06,082
What about me?

406
00:28:06,251 --> 00:28:09,449
Well, it appears that as
her closest surviving relative,

407
00:28:09,621 --> 00:28:11,351
- you get everything else.
- Like what?

408
00:28:11,523 --> 00:28:12,786
- Her clothes.
- Her clothes?

409
00:28:12,958 --> 00:28:14,893
I believe she had a car.

410
00:28:15,060 --> 00:28:16,426
Piece of junk.
What about jewels?

411
00:28:16,595 --> 00:28:17,995
I don't believe she had any.

412
00:28:18,163 --> 00:28:20,655
Stocks, bonds, mutual funds.

413
00:28:21,700 --> 00:28:23,794
When it came to
money, Mr. Lewis,

414
00:28:23,969 --> 00:28:25,801
your sister wasn't
much of a player.

415
00:28:26,772 --> 00:28:30,402
She was more the type who
believed the safest place for her money

416
00:28:31,443 --> 00:28:33,776
was under her mattress.

417
00:28:41,086 --> 00:28:42,918
Excuse me.

418
00:29:10,182 --> 00:29:12,310
They always crank
it up this loud?

419
00:29:12,484 --> 00:29:14,976
They think you
tourists like it that way.

420
00:29:15,387 --> 00:29:18,983
Not when we meet
people we wanna talk to.

421
00:29:20,592 --> 00:29:22,185
I know a place.

422
00:29:22,361 --> 00:29:24,125
- Can we dance there?
- You bet.

423
00:29:24,529 --> 00:29:25,963
Lead the way.

424
00:30:09,808 --> 00:30:12,004
What the hell are you doing?

425
00:30:13,278 --> 00:30:14,371
- Give me that.
- No way.

426
00:30:14,546 --> 00:30:15,707
Give me that. Carrie!

427
00:30:16,048 --> 00:30:18,347
- Game's over, Ashley.
- Carrie! Call the police.

428
00:30:18,517 --> 00:30:20,850
- Hey, what's going on? TIM:
They're setting you up, man.

429
00:30:21,019 --> 00:30:22,859
The minute you touch
her, she's gonna cry rape.

430
00:30:23,021 --> 00:30:24,114
Listen... Hey, hey!

431
00:30:24,289 --> 00:30:26,724
No! Give me that.

432
00:30:26,892 --> 00:30:27,916
No!

433
00:30:28,527 --> 00:30:29,586
- Hey!
- Get out of here!

434
00:30:32,864 --> 00:30:34,332
Get used to this face.

435
00:30:34,700 --> 00:30:36,965
It just gonna be in
yours from now on.

436
00:30:38,670 --> 00:30:40,798
What did he mean?
What is he talking about?

437
00:30:41,106 --> 00:30:42,699
Nothing. He's a nut case.

438
00:30:42,874 --> 00:30:45,708
I went out with him once, and
he's been harassing me ever since.

439
00:30:45,877 --> 00:30:47,573
- You should call the police.
- No.

440
00:30:47,746 --> 00:30:49,112
I don't want them involved.

441
00:30:49,281 --> 00:30:51,944
- But if the guy really is a nut case.
- Look, Jerry, ugh,

442
00:30:52,117 --> 00:30:54,712
it's sweet of you to be concerned,
but he's basically harmless.

443
00:30:54,886 --> 00:30:56,650
So just don't worry
about it, all right?

444
00:30:57,122 --> 00:30:58,750
Why don't we call it a night?

445
00:30:58,924 --> 00:31:01,189
- You sure you'll be okay?
- I'll be fine.

446
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:02,453
Where are you staying?

447
00:31:02,627 --> 00:31:04,926
Beach Side Inn. Room 211.

448
00:31:05,097 --> 00:31:06,998
I'll call you, okay?

449
00:31:07,833 --> 00:31:09,165
Good night.

450
00:31:09,334 --> 00:31:10,802
Good night.

451
00:31:16,007 --> 00:31:18,909
- What are you doing?
- Getting that geek off our backs.

452
00:31:19,077 --> 00:31:20,705
What do you think?

453
00:31:22,247 --> 00:31:23,943
Hi, it's Carrie.

454
00:31:24,116 --> 00:31:27,484
Yeah. Listen, remember that
favor I did for you last summer?

455
00:31:27,886 --> 00:31:30,287
Yeah, well, it's payback time.

456
00:31:59,317 --> 00:32:01,286
What is wrong?

457
00:32:19,638 --> 00:32:21,573
What...? Who is it...?

458
00:32:36,688 --> 00:32:37,712
What are you doing?

459
00:32:38,056 --> 00:32:39,615
- You're drowning.
- I am?

460
00:33:12,924 --> 00:33:15,621
Let's help him. - Let's go.

461
00:33:17,028 --> 00:33:18,519
Is he all right?

462
00:33:22,934 --> 00:33:25,995
- Keep his head up.
- Gotta get an ambulance.

463
00:33:33,278 --> 00:33:35,247
There's a phone
under my chair, call 911.

464
00:33:35,413 --> 00:33:37,143
Is he okay?

465
00:33:49,427 --> 00:33:52,090
Come on. MAN 1: Come on.

466
00:33:55,166 --> 00:33:56,634
Come on.

467
00:34:18,790 --> 00:34:20,691
It's no use. He's dead.

468
00:34:38,209 --> 00:34:41,702
Okay, everybody back.
Please, stand back.

469
00:34:46,184 --> 00:34:48,176
Oh, my God, that's Tim.

470
00:34:48,353 --> 00:34:49,821
You know him?

471
00:34:49,988 --> 00:34:51,012
Yeah, Tim McDaniels.

472
00:34:51,189 --> 00:34:53,715
He bought a board from
me a couple of days ago.

473
00:34:55,026 --> 00:34:57,621
Hey, Mike, what happened?

474
00:34:57,796 --> 00:35:00,459
Oh, man, he cramped
up or something.

475
00:35:00,732 --> 00:35:03,566
I could see he was in trouble,
but he was so far out, man.

476
00:35:03,735 --> 00:35:05,375
By the time I got to
him, it was too late.

477
00:35:05,537 --> 00:35:07,301
- He wasn't on his board?
- No.

478
00:35:07,472 --> 00:35:08,735
Well, why not?

479
00:35:08,907 --> 00:35:10,227
What am I, psychic?
How do I know?

480
00:35:10,375 --> 00:35:12,207
He went for a swim.

481
00:35:12,377 --> 00:35:14,217
All right, people, back
up. Excuse me. Back up.

482
00:35:14,379 --> 00:35:15,938
Let them through.

483
00:35:19,384 --> 00:35:20,909
Excuse me, folks.

484
00:35:21,086 --> 00:35:23,578
Please, everybody,
just keep back.

485
00:35:25,724 --> 00:35:27,522
I don't get it. I saw
him here the other day.

486
00:35:27,692 --> 00:35:29,888
The guy could swim like a fish.

487
00:35:30,195 --> 00:35:31,925
There are guys
out there on boards.

488
00:35:34,199 --> 00:35:36,668
Why didn't he call
for someone for help?

489
00:35:38,036 --> 00:35:40,096
It doesn't make sense.

490
00:35:47,579 --> 00:35:50,048
- Hi.
- Hi, Pop, where's Clyde?

491
00:35:50,215 --> 00:35:51,410
I left him on the beach.

492
00:35:51,583 --> 00:35:55,042
He's going swimming or running
or mountain climbing or something.

493
00:35:55,220 --> 00:35:57,382
I swear, for somebody
who doesn't eat sugar,

494
00:35:57,555 --> 00:35:59,615
he's got more energy
than anybody I ever saw.

495
00:35:59,791 --> 00:36:02,818
- Did you hear somebody drowned?
- Yeah. Clyde told me.

496
00:36:02,994 --> 00:36:04,622
He'd just sold him a board.

497
00:36:04,796 --> 00:36:07,527
Tim something.

498
00:36:08,366 --> 00:36:11,131
- Tim McDaniels?
- Yeah.

499
00:36:11,302 --> 00:36:13,828
- We met him.
- Yeah. Clyde just introduced us.

500
00:36:14,005 --> 00:36:15,885
Oh, we had a really weird
conversation with him.

501
00:36:16,041 --> 00:36:18,772
- Huh?
- Yeah. It seems some, quote-unquote

502
00:36:18,943 --> 00:36:21,708
"friend of his" was falsely accused
of date rape by some woman.

503
00:36:21,880 --> 00:36:24,782
The guy figured he'd better pay her
off so she wouldn't go to the police.

504
00:36:24,949 --> 00:36:27,282
He wanted to know what
legal recourse his friend had.

505
00:36:27,452 --> 00:36:29,444
And we figured he was
talking about himself.

506
00:36:29,621 --> 00:36:31,852
Of course he was
talking about himself.

507
00:36:35,660 --> 00:36:37,822
- Dad, where are you going?
- Library.

508
00:36:37,996 --> 00:36:39,328
Read a little.

509
00:36:55,980 --> 00:36:57,414
Hi.

510
00:36:57,582 --> 00:36:59,915
Leanne Mclntyre.

511
00:37:00,618 --> 00:37:02,018
Mike Rydell.

512
00:37:06,758 --> 00:37:10,217
Guess you've had a pretty
difficult day, haven't you?

513
00:37:12,097 --> 00:37:13,156
Yeah.

514
00:37:13,331 --> 00:37:16,995
I'm just staying down the
beach at Lucy Lewis' place.

515
00:37:17,302 --> 00:37:19,794
- I heard she passed away.
- Did you know her?

516
00:37:21,539 --> 00:37:24,338
I had to rescue her
a couple of times.

517
00:37:24,509 --> 00:37:25,636
First time was legit.

518
00:37:25,810 --> 00:37:27,642
She fell off her board,
couldn't get back in.

519
00:37:27,812 --> 00:37:31,340
But the other times... I think
she just liked the first time a lot.

520
00:37:33,718 --> 00:37:35,346
- Was she a relative of yours?
- No.

521
00:37:35,520 --> 00:37:37,887
My dad and I just
came in for the funeral.

522
00:37:38,056 --> 00:37:40,389
Oh, yeah, the lawyers. Right?

523
00:37:40,558 --> 00:37:41,719
I heard about you guys.

524
00:37:41,893 --> 00:37:43,020
Well, listen, I gotta go.

525
00:37:44,863 --> 00:37:47,765
So, what happened today, anyway?

526
00:37:48,166 --> 00:37:50,328
Well, some guy got himself
into trouble and drowned.

527
00:37:50,502 --> 00:37:51,970
Did you know him?

528
00:37:52,137 --> 00:37:54,470
I'd seen him around.
Not personally, no.

529
00:37:54,639 --> 00:37:56,608
Heard he was a good swimmer.

530
00:37:56,775 --> 00:37:59,438
Yeah. Most surfers are. He
cramped up or something.

531
00:37:59,611 --> 00:38:02,171
Was he unconscious
when you got to him?

532
00:38:02,347 --> 00:38:04,077
Just about.

533
00:38:14,392 --> 00:38:15,883
Someone on the beach

534
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:18,529
told the police it looked like
you were struggling with him.

535
00:38:18,696 --> 00:38:21,962
Look, I don't care what some
turkey standing 100 yards away

536
00:38:22,133 --> 00:38:23,999
thinks he saw in
the surf. I was there.

537
00:38:24,169 --> 00:38:27,264
The guy was blue and starting to sink
when I got to him, and he grabbed me,

538
00:38:27,438 --> 00:38:31,967
pulled me under and was unconscious
by the time I got him out. Okay?

539
00:38:36,514 --> 00:38:38,483
And how do you know what
somebody told the cops?

540
00:38:38,650 --> 00:38:41,984
I had heard that the guy that
drowned was having personal problems.

541
00:38:42,153 --> 00:38:43,621
Now he's dead.

542
00:38:43,788 --> 00:38:46,019
That's strange. And I'm curious.

543
00:38:46,191 --> 00:38:47,750
Yeah.

544
00:38:48,126 --> 00:38:51,824
Well, it has been
a long, rotten day,

545
00:38:51,996 --> 00:38:53,862
and I don't feel like
talking about it anymore.

546
00:38:59,304 --> 00:39:00,966
Damn it!

547
00:39:03,675 --> 00:39:04,802
Excuse me.

548
00:39:21,960 --> 00:39:25,226
So she asked you some
questions. What's the big deal?

549
00:39:26,397 --> 00:39:28,628
Look, I don't care if she's
related to J. Edgar Hoover.

550
00:39:28,800 --> 00:39:33,101
Neither she nor anybody else is
ever gonna be able to prove a thing.

551
00:39:34,572 --> 00:39:36,541
Mike, chill out.

552
00:39:36,708 --> 00:39:39,007
Everything is fine.

553
00:39:39,310 --> 00:39:41,609
No. Just go have a
beer, watch some tube.

554
00:39:41,779 --> 00:39:44,806
Get some sleep, and I'll
talk to you tomorrow, okay?

555
00:39:46,517 --> 00:39:50,079
Attaboy. Sweet dreams.

556
00:39:51,756 --> 00:39:53,349
What was that all about?

557
00:39:53,524 --> 00:39:57,052
Some lawyer's been asking
Mike questions about the drowning.

558
00:39:57,562 --> 00:39:59,326
Why would some lawyer
be asking questions?

559
00:39:59,497 --> 00:40:02,899
Who cares? Nobody's gonna
be able to prove anything.

560
00:40:03,067 --> 00:40:05,468
We're home free.
Why can't he see that?

561
00:40:05,637 --> 00:40:07,230
Because he's got
the IQ of a grape.

562
00:40:07,672 --> 00:40:10,039
I should have never
asked him to do it.

563
00:40:10,375 --> 00:40:12,435
Can I ask you a question?

564
00:40:12,810 --> 00:40:14,005
Why did he say yes?

565
00:40:14,913 --> 00:40:16,142
He owed me.

566
00:40:18,516 --> 00:40:21,076
Helped him duck a possession
with intent to sell charge last year

567
00:40:21,252 --> 00:40:22,413
by lying to the cops.

568
00:40:26,090 --> 00:40:28,389
Thought he could
handle something like this.

569
00:40:29,260 --> 00:40:30,660
Think he's gonna cave?

570
00:40:31,562 --> 00:40:33,155
I don't know.

571
00:40:35,533 --> 00:40:37,001
French braid my hair, okay?

572
00:40:37,168 --> 00:40:39,296
- Now?
- Yeah.

573
00:40:39,637 --> 00:40:42,732
I do my best thinking when
I'm getting my hair done.

574
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:57,782
Hi.

575
00:40:58,323 --> 00:40:59,347
Hi.

576
00:41:00,425 --> 00:41:01,859
Feeling better?

577
00:41:03,661 --> 00:41:05,493
No. Not really.

578
00:41:16,541 --> 00:41:18,134
- I shouldn't have done it.
- Yes.

579
00:41:18,309 --> 00:41:20,972
You should have done it.
You owe me, remember?

580
00:41:21,679 --> 00:41:24,513
Look, I told you last night. Nobody's
gonna be able to prove anything.

581
00:41:24,682 --> 00:41:26,981
Oh, that's easy for you to say.

582
00:41:27,251 --> 00:41:30,050
Mike, the guy drowned.
Just like you said.

583
00:41:30,221 --> 00:41:31,712
There's no evidence
for anything else.

584
00:41:31,889 --> 00:41:35,257
Carrie, I got Ben Matlock's daughter
asking me questions, talking to police.

585
00:41:35,426 --> 00:41:37,327
I'm sure when they talk,
that don't get ignored.

586
00:41:37,495 --> 00:41:41,193
- Look, this isn't the place.
- Hey, you brought it up, okay?

587
00:41:46,304 --> 00:41:47,772
Look.

588
00:41:47,939 --> 00:41:50,272
I'll come over tonight, okay?

589
00:41:50,541 --> 00:41:52,476
We can talk about it.

590
00:41:53,277 --> 00:41:54,768
Fine.

591
00:41:57,715 --> 00:42:00,742
Mike, one hand
washes the other one.

592
00:42:01,152 --> 00:42:02,552
You want some money?

593
00:42:02,720 --> 00:42:05,212
You want to split?
I'll make it right.

594
00:42:05,390 --> 00:42:07,655
Anything you want, okay?

595
00:42:08,126 --> 00:42:09,822
I'll take care of it.

596
00:42:11,062 --> 00:42:12,587
All right.

597
00:42:13,231 --> 00:42:14,893
I mean it.

598
00:42:16,467 --> 00:42:19,926
Well, I guess we can all feel
safe, knowing you're on the job.

599
00:42:24,575 --> 00:42:26,840
- Get lost. All right?
- Hey, Mike, I got an idea.

600
00:42:27,011 --> 00:42:29,606
Why don't you try watching the
water instead of the girls, huh?

601
00:42:29,781 --> 00:42:31,909
- Hey, you got a problem?
- Yeah. Maybe that way

602
00:42:32,083 --> 00:42:35,019
the tourists wouldn't have a bad
habit of coming out of the water dead.

603
00:42:37,121 --> 00:42:38,885
- Stop it.
- On my beach, what I say goes,

604
00:42:39,190 --> 00:42:41,716
and I say get lost. Now move it.

605
00:42:45,963 --> 00:42:48,125
You're not worth it, Rydell.

606
00:43:00,711 --> 00:43:02,509
How about eggs?

607
00:43:02,680 --> 00:43:03,841
Yup. We got eggs.

608
00:43:04,182 --> 00:43:07,016
- There you go.
- Thanks.

609
00:43:07,452 --> 00:43:10,616
You know, I feel so silly. I
thought borrowing a cup of sugar

610
00:43:10,788 --> 00:43:12,508
was something people
only did in the movies.

611
00:43:16,294 --> 00:43:18,263
Carrie and I just got the
urge to bake something.

612
00:43:18,429 --> 00:43:19,488
That ever happen to you?

613
00:43:19,664 --> 00:43:22,065
Oh, yes. I used to love to bake.

614
00:43:22,233 --> 00:43:25,897
Of course, that was back
in the '70s, in the old days,

615
00:43:26,070 --> 00:43:27,310
when the main ingredient was...

616
00:43:31,742 --> 00:43:33,643
Carrie, would you get that?

617
00:43:33,811 --> 00:43:35,074
Carrie?

618
00:43:37,815 --> 00:43:39,615
<i>We're not here right now.</i>

619
00:43:39,750 --> 00:43:41,070
<i>If you leave your
name and number,</i>

620
00:43:41,219 --> 00:43:43,347
<i>we'll get back to you
as soon as we can.</i>

621
00:43:43,955 --> 00:43:45,287
She got it.

622
00:43:45,456 --> 00:43:48,119
Well, I gotta get
going, thanks a lot.

623
00:43:48,459 --> 00:43:49,950
Yeah.

624
00:44:19,457 --> 00:44:21,323
I just don't know.

625
00:44:23,661 --> 00:44:26,358
Feels to me like you
could use a back rub.

626
00:44:29,867 --> 00:44:32,530
- Come on.
- Okay.

627
00:45:01,966 --> 00:45:03,457
I'm ready.

628
00:45:05,436 --> 00:45:07,166
Mm.

629
00:45:08,206 --> 00:45:10,198
Oh.

630
00:45:11,209 --> 00:45:13,110
Feels great.

631
00:45:28,759 --> 00:45:30,557
That's great.

632
00:45:39,704 --> 00:45:41,036
Agh.

633
00:46:45,803 --> 00:46:49,001
Hello? CARRIE: <i>It's over.</i>

634
00:46:51,275 --> 00:46:53,335
So am I enjoying dinner?

635
00:46:53,511 --> 00:46:55,002
<i>We're eating barbecue chicken.</i>

636
00:46:55,179 --> 00:46:58,513
Your favorite. We're about to have the
marble cake we just made for dessert.

637
00:47:06,324 --> 00:47:08,384
Perfect.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
